  "problem": "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+5x-6,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)",
  "result": "Incorrect",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n$\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 5 & -6 \\\\  &  & 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\  & 1 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$\\frac{f(x)}{x-2}={x}^{2}-x+3$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$f(x)=(x-2)({x}^{2}-x+3).$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}-x+3=0$ is \n\n$D=(1{)}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-11<0,$\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n$\\begin{aligned}y & ={0}^{3}-3(0{)}^{2}+5(0)-6=-6.\\end{aligned}$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\nhas a $y$-intercept at $(0,-6),$\n\npasses through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\nhas no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $S$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](https://mathacademy.com/graphics/q-39032-a-1)",
